{"config":{"lang":["en"],"separator":"[\\s\\-]+","pipeline":["stopWordFilter"],"fields":{"title":{"boost":1000.0},"text":{"boost":1.0},"tags":{"boost":1000000.0}}},"docs":[{"location":"open-textbooks/wb-principles-of-economics/index.html","title":"Overview and preface","text":"Open TextbooksPrinciples of EconomicsA brief introductionWikibooks contributors <p>Open Textbooks</p> Principles of Economics <p>A brief introduction</p> <p>Wikibooks contributors</p> <p>Scarcity, supply and demand, utility and elasticity, the costs of firms, and the basic models of macroeconomics.</p> Start reading <p>48 chapters1 appendix</p>"},{"location":"open-textbooks/wb-principles-of-economics/index.html#contents","title":"Contents","text":"<p>Preface</p> <ul> <li>Section 1: To the Student</li> <li>Section 2: What is Economics?</li> <li>Section 3: What kind of economist are you?</li> </ul> <p>Introduction</p> <ul> <li>Section 4: Scarcity</li> <li>Section 5: Economic Modeling</li> <li>Section 6: Assumptions Economists Make</li> <li>Section 7: Allocation</li> <li>Section 8: Perfect Competition</li> <li>Section 9: Cautions</li> <li>Section 10: Specialization and Gains From Trade</li> <li>Section 11: Production Possibilities</li> <li>Section 12: Demand</li> <li>Section 13: Supply</li> </ul> <p>Microeconomics</p> <ul> <li>Section 14: Utility</li> <li>Section 15: Marginal Utility</li> <li>Section 16: Opportunity Costs</li> <li>Section 17: Demand Laws</li> <li>Section 18: Elasticity</li> <li>Section 19: Economic Systems</li> </ul> <p>Models</p> <ul> <li>Section 20: Graphs</li> <li>Section 21: Budget Constraints</li> <li>Section 22: Production Possibilities</li> <li>Section 23: Indifference</li> <li>Section 24: Types of Goods</li> <li>Section 25: Budget Compensation</li> <li>Section 26: Trade and Specialization</li> <li>Section 27: Labor Production</li> <li>Section 28: Wage</li> <li>Section 29: Costs to Firm</li> <li>Section 30: Economies of Scale</li> <li>Section 31: Supply and Demand</li> <li>Section 32: S-D Shifts</li> </ul> <p>Macroeconomics</p> <ul> <li>Section 33: Macro objectives</li> <li>Section 34: GDP</li> <li>Section 35: Multipliers</li> <li>Section 36: Money Supply</li> <li>Section 37: Money</li> <li>Section 38: Monetary Policy</li> <li>Section 39: Fiscal Policy</li> <li>Section 40: Taxation</li> <li>Section 41: Interest Rates</li> </ul> <p>Models</p> <ul> <li>Section 42: Laffer Curve</li> <li>Section 43: Keynesian Cross Model</li> <li>Section 44: Aggregate Supply &amp; Demand Model (ASAD)</li> <li>Section 45: Liquidity Trap Money Model</li> <li>Section 46: Investment Savings Loan Money model (ISLM)</li> <li>Section 47: Philips Curve</li> <li>Section 48: Back-ward Bending Labor Supply Curve</li> </ul> <p>Appendices</p> <ul> <li>Appendix A: Special contributions to this work</li> </ul>"},{"location":"open-textbooks/wb-principles-of-economics/index.html#about-this-book","title":"About this book","text":"<p>A short first course in economics with no prerequisites: thinking like an economist, scarcity and opportunity cost, gains from trade, supply and demand, utility and elasticity, budget constraints and indifference curves, labour, wages and the costs of firms, and in macroeconomics GDP, multipliers, money, monetary and fiscal policy, taxation and interest rates, with the Keynesian cross, AS-AD, IS-LM and Phillips curve models.</p> <p>From Wikibooks · CC BY-SA 4.0</p><p>Principles of Economics, a Wikibooks textbook, by its contributors. This book is not stored in this library: each chapter is read from Wikibooks when it is opened, shown in this library's format, and ends with its own attribution. Licence: CC BY-SA 4.0. Modified: converted to this format.</p>"},{"location":"open-textbooks/wb-principles-of-economics/allocation.html","title":"Section 7: Allocation","text":""},{"location":"open-textbooks/wb-principles-of-economics/allocation.html#71-scarcity-allocation-and-markets","title":"7.1 Scarcity, Allocation, and Markets","text":""},{"location